/E (METHODOLOGY)
/Pg 11 0 R
/K [11]
>>
endobj
185 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 146 0 R
/E (The objective of this project is to build a resume-job matching tool that understands the meaning of text rather than just matching exact words. By using a pre-trained language model to convert resumes and job descriptions into embeddings, the system compares them semantically and generates a match score. This helps recognize equivalent skills expressed in different wording, giving qualified candidates a fairer chance.)
/Pg 11 0 R
/K [12]
>>
endobj
186 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H4
/P 582 0 R
/T (Convert resume and job description into embeddings using a pre-trained model \(e.g., Sentence-BERT\))
/E (Convert resume and job description into embeddings using a pre-trained model \(e.g., Sentence-BERT\))
/Pg 11 0 R
/K [13]
>>
endobj
187 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H4
/P 583 0 R
/T (Compare them with cosine similarity to get a match score)
/E (Compare them with cosine similarity to get a match score)
/Pg 11 0 R
/K [14]
>>
endobj
188 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H4
/P 584 0 R
/T (Benchmark against a keyword-matching baseline)
/E (Benchmark against a keyword-matching baseline)
/Pg 11 0 R
/K [15]
>>
endobj
189 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 146 0 R
/E (Our semantic matcher stayed consistent when applicants rewrote the same qualifications in different words, while TF-IDF scores dropped by nearly half. This shows that embedding-based matching recognizes equivalent skills that keyword matching misses, so qualified engineers stay shortlisted. Engineering fixes such as sliding-window chunk pooling, substance scaling, and domain alignment checks )
/Pg 11 0 R
/K [16]
>>
endobj
